{"patch":{"config":{"title":"Antranig Basman","baseURL":"https://ponder.org.uk","defaultContentLanguage":"en","mainSections":["post","posts"],"author":{"name":"Antranig Basman","website":"/","email":"amb26web@ponder.org.uk","twitter":"amb26ponder"},"params":{"homeTitle":"Antranig Basman","subtitle":"Building habitable technologies","bio":"img/20230209_135356 - bio - cropped.jpg","logo":"img/pond.jpg","favicon":"img/favicon.ico","dateFormat":"January 2, 2006","comments":true,"socialShare":true,"showRelatedPosts":true,"commentsLayer":"beautifulWend.comments.giscus","customCSS":["css/ponder.css"],"mediaIcons":[{"title":"Twitter profile","href":"http://twitter.com/amb26ponder","icon":"fab fa-twitter"},{"title":"Send me a message","href":"mailto:amb26webpage@ponder.org.uk","icon":"fa fa-envelope"},{"title":"Antranig's GitHub profile","href":"http://github.com/amb26","icon":"fab fa-github-alt"},{"title":"My papers on DBLP","href":"https://dblp.org/pid/68/5013.html","icon":"fa fa-university"},{"title":"My LinkedIn profile","href":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/antranigbasman/","icon":"fab fa-linkedin"}],"giscus":{"repo":"amb26/ponder","repoId":"R_kgDOOKllGA","category":"Announcements","categoryId":"DIC_kwDOOKllGM4CvhAS"}},"menu":{"main":[{"name":"Posts","url":"post","weight":1},{"name":"WiP","url":"wip","weight":1},{"name":"Docs","identifier":"docs","url":"docs","weight":3},{"name":"Fluid Signals","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-signals","weight":1},{"name":"Fluid Integration Language","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-integration-language","weight":2},{"name":"Fluid View","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-view","weight":3},{"name":"Fluid PTF","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-ptf","weight":4},{"name":"Fluid tldraw integration","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-tldraw","weight":5},{"name":"wend","parent":"docs","url":"docs/wend","weight":6},{"name":"About","url":"page/about/","weight":3},{"name":"Communities","url":"communities","weight":4},{"name":"Projects","url":"projects","weight":5},{"name":"Terms","url":"term","weight":5}]}},"features":{"selfHosted":true,"hljs":true}},"pages":[{"kind":"page","type":"wip","section":"wip","title":"Templates and weird attributes together","date":"2025-04-07T00:00:00.000Z","relPermalink":"/wip/2025-04-07-templates-and-weird-attributes-together/","permalink":"https://ponder.org.uk/wip/2025-04-07-templates-and-weird-attributes-together/","outputPath":"/wip/2025-04-07-templates-and-weird-attributes-together/index.html","params":{"title":"Templates and weird attributes together","date":"2025-04-07"},"sourcePath":"content/wip/2025-04-07-templates-and-weird-attributes-together.md","content":"<p>Trying to put into practice thoughts from the previous two postings on <a href=\"/wip/2025-04-03-change-of-tack-on-templating\">template thinking</a>\nand <a href=\"/wip/2025-04-04-staring-at-the-wand\">weird attributes</a> for event binding revealed some interesting problems.</p>\n<p>Firing up <code>${ }</code>-based templates revealed an awkward problem. This prevents us using\nbacktick template literals in code since these automatically interpret <code>${}</code> syntax and so will break as soon\nas they are evaluated.</p>\n<p>At the same time, use of <code>@click</code> style event binding revealed a really interesting limitation. Whilst it is perfect\npossible to parse overall HTML documents containing weird attributes like these, <em>and</em> it is possible to assign them\ninto the document in bulk using <code>innerHTML</code> or <code>createContextualFragment</code> these may <em>not</em> be set on individual nodes using a\ncall to <code>node.setAttribute</code>. There is an amazing inconsistency that the latter is subject to a special\ncheck leading to an error <code>@click is not a valid attribute name</code>. Which it is not, of course –\n<a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/925994/what-characters-are-allowed-in-an-html-attribute-name\">this posting</a> holds\na pointer to the documentation on a valid <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/xml/#NT-Name\">XML name</a> which shows these are highly\nrestricted. They can only begin with a colon (boo!), underscore or letter character.</p>\n<p>Which makes it amazing that there are so many routes for getting such invalid attribute names into the document in the\nfirst place, and supplies a clue to why Vue supports two syntaxes (<code>v-on:click</code> and <code>@click</code>).</p>\n<p>However, once we fixed up the obviously broken logic, the test cases pass again leading us to ponder that there is really\nno need to manipulate such attributes in the DOM in the first place as long as the standard routes for getting bulk\nmarkup into the DOM for templating are workable. Who knows exactly what the original thinking in Vue was but it makes\na lot of sense for us to support just one syntax. DOM reconciliation only needs to work for properly extended material –\nstuff actually forming the live part of the UI.</p>\n<p>Leaving the original templating issue on the table. Given we shirk <code>{{}}</code> because we want to render Hugo templates,\nand shirk <code>${}</code> because we want to use backticks in code, our attribute syntax suggests we go for <code>@{}</code> which\nwhilst a bit funny is not unheard of – looks like the most popular second choice is Ruby’s <code>#{}</code>.\nWe want something delimited since the original Infusion syntax based on <code>%name</code>\nwas annoying based on not wanting to restrict the character set which appeared in interpolated keys.</p>\n<p>But the pair of choices based on <code>@key</code> in attribute names and <code>@{}</code> in text seem to work well together – a clear visual\nindicator that interpolated material in markup begins with <code>@</code> as against interpolated material in JSON beginning\nwith <code>$</code>.</p>\n","summary":"Trying to put into practice thoughts from the previous two postings on template thinking and weird attributes for event binding revealed some interesting problems. Firing up ${ } -based templates revealed an awkward problem. This prevents us using backtick template literals in code since these automatically interpret ${} syntax and so will break as soon as they are evaluated. At the same time, use of @click style event binding revealed a really interesting limitation.","summaryText":"Trying to put into practice thoughts from the previous two postings on template thinking and weird attributes for event binding revealed some interesting problems. Firing up ${ } -based templates revealed an awkward problem. This prevents us using backtick template literals in code since these automatically interpret ${} syntax and so will break as soon as they are evaluated. At the same time, use of @click style event binding revealed a really interesting limitation.","truncated":true,"prevInSection":{"title":"Staring at the wand","relPermalink":"/wip/2025-04-04-staring-at-the-wand/"},"nextInSection":{"title":"Co-occurrences are here again","relPermalink":"/wip/2025-04-21-co-occurrences-are-here-again/"}}],"shared":{"lang":"en","menu":[{"name":"Posts","url":"post","weight":1,"children":[]},{"name":"WiP","url":"wip","weight":1,"children":[]},{"name":"About","url":"page/about/","weight":3,"children":[]},{"name":"Docs","identifier":"docs","url":"docs","weight":3,"children":[{"name":"Fluid Signals","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-signals","weight":1,"children":[]},{"name":"Fluid Integration Language","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-integration-language","weight":2,"children":[]},{"name":"Fluid View","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-view","weight":3,"children":[]},{"name":"Fluid PTF","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-ptf","weight":4,"children":[]},{"name":"Fluid tldraw integration","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-tldraw","weight":5,"children":[]},{"name":"wend","parent":"docs","url":"docs/wend","weight":6,"children":[]}]},{"name":"Communities","url":"communities","weight":4,"children":[]},{"name":"Projects","url":"projects","we